● MCP Architecture

  Overview

  MCP follows a client-server architecture where AI applications (clients) communicate with external services (servers) through a standardized protocol.

  ┌──────────────┐         ┌──────────────┐
  │  AI Model    │         │  MCP Server  │  (GitHub, Slack, DB, etc.)
  │  (Claude)    │         │              │
  └──────┬───────┘         └──────▲───────┘
         │                        │
  ┌──────▼───────┐         ┌──────┴───────┐
  │  MCP Client  │◄───────►│  Transport   │
  │  (Host App)  │ JSON-RPC│  (stdio/HTTP)│
  └──────────────┘         └──────────────┘

  Core Components

  1. Host — The AI application (e.g., Claude Code, Claude Desktop). It manages one or more MCP clients and controls permissions.

  2. Client — Lives inside the host. Each client maintains a 1:1 connection with a single MCP server. Handles protocol negotiation and message routing.

  3. Server — An external program that exposes three types of capabilities:
  - Tools — Functions the AI can invoke (e.g., create_issue, query_database)
  - Resources — Read-only data the AI can access (e.g., file contents, API data)
  - Prompts — Reusable prompt templates for common workflows

  Communication Protocol

  - Uses JSON-RPC 2.0 for message format
  - Supports two transport layers:
    - stdio — For local servers (spawned as child processes)
    - HTTP + SSE (Server-Sent Events) — For remote servers

  Connection Lifecycle

  1. Initialization — Client sends initialize request; server responds with its capabilities
  2. Capability negotiation — Both sides agree on supported features
  3. Normal operation — Client sends requests, server responds; server can also send notifications
  4. Shutdown — Graceful disconnection

  Security Model

  - The user always approves tool calls before execution
  - Servers declare what tools/resources they offer; the host decides what to expose to the AI
  - Servers run with limited permissions — they can't access things outside their scope

  Multi-Server Setup

  A single host can connect to multiple servers simultaneously:

  Claude Code (Host)
    ├── Client ↔ GitHub Server
    ├── Client ↔ Slack Server
    ├── Client ↔ Database Server
    └── Client ↔ Filesystem Server

  This composability is one of MCP's biggest strengths — the AI can use tools from all connected servers in a single conversation.

  Why This Architecture?

  ┌────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │     Design Choice      │                       Benefit                        │
  ├────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Client-server          │ Separation of concerns; servers are reusable         │
  ├────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ JSON-RPC               │ Lightweight, well-understood standard                │
  ├────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ 1:1 client-server      │ Isolation; one server crashing doesn't affect others │
  ├────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Capability negotiation │ Forward compatibility as the protocol evolves        │
  ├────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ User approval          │ Security without sacrificing flexibility             │
  └────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

