● MCP Architecture — Deep Dive

  ---
  1. Transport Layer

  MCP supports two transport mechanisms. The protocol itself is transport-agnostic — the same JSON-RPC messages work over either.

  stdio (Local Servers)

  Host Process
    │
    ├── spawns server as child process
    │
    ├── stdin  ──► Server reads requests
    └── stdout ◄── Server writes responses
        stderr ◄── Server logs/diagnostics (not part of protocol)

  - The host spawns the server as a subprocess
  - Messages flow over stdin/stdout pipes
  - Each message is delimited by newlines
  - Simple, fast, no network overhead
  - Server dies when the host disconnects
  - Best for: Local tools like file systems, databases, CLI wrappers

  HTTP + SSE (Remote Servers)

  Client ──HTTP POST──► Server    (client-to-server requests)
  Client ◄──SSE stream── Server   (server-to-client responses & notifications)

  - Client sends requests via HTTP POST to the server's endpoint
  - Server pushes responses and notifications back via Server-Sent Events (SSE)
  - SSE provides a persistent, one-way stream from server to client
  - Supports authentication headers (OAuth, API keys)
  - Best for: Cloud-hosted servers, shared services, remote APIs

  Why SSE instead of WebSockets?

  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    SSE                     │             WebSockets              │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Works through firewalls/proxies easily     │ Can be blocked by corporate proxies │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Built on standard HTTP                     │ Requires protocol upgrade           │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Simpler to implement                       │ More complex handshake              │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ One-way stream + HTTP POST = bidirectional │ Native bidirectional                │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Auto-reconnection built in                 │ Manual reconnection logic needed    │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────┘

  ---
  2. Protocol Messages (JSON-RPC 2.0)

  All MCP communication uses JSON-RPC 2.0. There are three message types:

  Requests (client → server or server → client)

  {
    "jsonrpc": "2.0",
    "id": 1,
    "method": "tools/call",
    "params": {
      "name": "create_issue",
      "arguments": { "title": "Bug fix", "body": "Details..." }
    }
  }

  Responses

  {
    "jsonrpc": "2.0",
    "id": 1,
    "result": {
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Issue #42 created" }]
    }
  }

  Notifications (no id, no response expected)

  {
    "jsonrpc": "2.0",
    "method": "notifications/resources/updated",
    "params": { "uri": "file:///data/config.json" }
  }

  Connection Lifecycle in Detail

  Client                              Server
    │                                    │
    ├──── initialize ───────────────────►│  (send client capabilities)
    │◄─── initialize response ──────────┤  (server capabilities back)
    │                                    │
    ├──── notifications/initialized ────►│  (handshake complete)
    │                                    │
    │         === Normal Operation ===   │
    │                                    │
    ├──── tools/list ───────────────────►│  (discover available tools)
    │◄─── tool list response ───────────┤
    │                                    │
    ├──── tools/call ───────────────────►│  (invoke a tool)
    │◄─── tool result ──────────────────┤
    │                                    │
    ├──── resources/read ───────────────►│  (read a resource)
    │◄─── resource content ─────────────┤
    │                                    │
    │◄─── notifications/resources/updated│  (server pushes updates)
    │                                    │
    ├──── shutdown ─────────────────────►│  (graceful disconnect)
    │                                    │

  Key Methods

  ┌─────────────────┬──────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │     Method      │    Direction     │                 Purpose                 │
  ├─────────────────┼──────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ initialize      │ Client → Server  │ Start connection, exchange capabilities │
  ├─────────────────┼──────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ tools/list      │ Client → Server  │ Discover available tools                │
  ├─────────────────┼──────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ tools/call      │ Client → Server  │ Execute a tool                          │
  ├─────────────────┼──────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ resources/list  │ Client → Server  │ List available resources                │
  ├─────────────────┼──────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ resources/read  │ Client → Server  │ Read a resource's content               │
  ├─────────────────┼──────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ prompts/list    │ Client → Server  │ List prompt templates                   │
  ├─────────────────┼──────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ prompts/get     │ Client → Server  │ Get a specific prompt                   │
  ├─────────────────┼──────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ notifications/* │ Either direction │ Async events (no response needed)       │
  └─────────────────┴──────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ---
  3. Security Model

  MCP has a layered security design with multiple trust boundaries.

  Trust Boundaries

  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  User                                        │
  │  (ultimate authority — approves everything)   │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
  │  │  Host (Claude Code / Claude Desktop)    │  │
  │  │  - Enforces user consent                │  │
  │  │  - Controls what servers connect        │  │
  │  │  - Filters what tools AI can see        │  │
  │  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────┐   │  │
  │  │  │  Client                          │   │  │
  │  │  │  - 1:1 with a server             │   │  │
  │  │  │  - Mediates all communication    │   │  │
  │  │  └────────────┬────────────────────┘   │  │
  │  └───────────────┼────────────────────────┘  │
  └──────────────────┼───────────────────────────┘
                     │ (trust boundary)
     ┌───────────────▼────────────────────┐
     │  MCP Server                         │
     │  - Runs with minimal permissions    │
     │  - Cannot access other servers      │
     │  - Declares capabilities upfront    │
     └────────────────────────────────────┘

  User Approval Flow

  1. AI decides a tool call would help → "I should query the database"
  2. Host presents the call to the user →
     ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
     │  MCP Server wants to run:        │
     │  tool: query_database            │
     │  args: { "sql": "SELECT ..." }   │
     │                                   │
     │  [Allow]  [Allow Always]  [Deny] │
     └──────────────────────────────────┘
  3. User approves → tool executes
  4. Result returns to AI → AI uses it in response

  Key Security Principles

  - Principle of least privilege — Servers only get access to what they need. A GitHub server can't read your filesystem.
  - Server isolation — Each server runs independently. A compromised server can't affect others.
  - No ambient authority — Tools don't auto-execute. Every call requires explicit or pre-configured user approval.
  - Capability declaration — Servers announce what they can do during initialize. No hidden functionality.
  - Input validation — Servers should validate all inputs from the AI (the AI is untrusted from the server's perspective).
  - Prompt injection defense — Tool results could contain malicious instructions. The host should treat tool outputs as untrusted data.

  Permission Modes (in Claude Code)

  ┌───────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │       Mode        │                       Behavior                        │
  ├───────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Ask every time    │ User confirms each tool call                          │
  ├───────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Allow per session │ Once approved, same tool auto-allowed for the session │
  ├───────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Allow always      │ Tool permanently allowed (saved in settings)          │
  ├───────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Deny              │ Block the tool call                                   │
  └───────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

